[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Verification shall cover immediate independent alarms, source quality,
PM units/windows, multi-detector latches, positive HEAL, restart/time faults,
bounded deadlines, guidance/output conflicts, persistence and notification/UI
contracts as detailed in the feature architecture.

---
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-8-8
TITLE: Functional Safety and Platform Health Monitoring (C-FSM)

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Scope:** C-FSM supports HARA `HZ‑SYSTEM‑FAIL‑01` / `SG‑003` by supervising
whether the SafetyFunctions application, Home Assistant, and their host can
continue to observe, evaluate, and report safety conditions. It is distinct
from the household hazard state and from C-ENT's per-entity quality checks.
The [Safety Monitoring Responsibility Boundaries architecture](<../features/Safety Monitoring Responsibility Boundaries.md>)
defines monitor ownership and observer placement.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-001
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall present hazard/fault state, safety-function coverage, and reporting-channel health separately. `no_faults` shall not assert that all functions were evaluated or that all reporting paths work.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-002
STATEMENT: >>>
SafetyFunctions shall expose startup and configuration validity, and the last completed evaluation and result for each enabled Safety Component. Periodic components shall additionally expose their deadline and missed-deadline count; event-driven components shall identify their trigger mode and rely on declared input-health supervision during idle periods. A component with no completed evaluation or an overdue periodic evaluation shall not be presented as healthy. Application and MQTT heartbeats alone shall not establish component coverage. For startup-capable progress reports, the frontend shall display the shared diagnostic startup phase and countdown globally in both operator modes until current valid backend evidence confirms completion; malformed, stale or unavailable supplied startup metadata shall be distinguished from completion. Legacy progress reports without startup metadata shall remain compatible without asserting a startup phase.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-003
STATEMENT: >>>
In-process checks shall not claim to detect complete loss of SafetyFunctions, Home Assistant, or the host. Such failures require an independent observer and reporting path; when neither is installed, the system shall identify this boundary as uncovered rather than claim continued monitoring.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-004
STATEMENT: >>>
The platform monitor shall use validated Home Assistant or Supervisor telemetry for available memory, memory pressure, CPU, disk, swap, host temperature, restart history, and connectivity. The observation shall identify whether it measures the HA host or only an App container; container-only memory shall not be accepted as host-memory evidence. A missing, stale, malformed, or wrong-unit metric shall produce unknown coverage, not positive host-health evidence.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-005
STATEMENT: >>>
Sustained insufficient memory available to Home Assistant, confirmed by memory pressure/PSI, shall produce a level-2 platform-health fault after calibrated qualification. Both measurements are required; if either is unsupported or unavailable the rule shall be reported as uncovered, not silently reduced to one signal. Clearing shall require fresh valid recovery evidence beyond a separate hysteresis margin; an isolated high-use sample shall not assert or clear the fault.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-006
STATEMENT: >>>
Sustained CPU, storage, swap, and thermal pressure shall be diagnosed separately and correlated with missed safety-evaluation deadlines. Qualified CPU pressure shall produce a level-3 warning. Resource pressure alone shall not be presented as proof that a safety decision was missed.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-007
STATEMENT: >>>
Confirmed Internet/WAN loss shall create a level-3 network fault, separately from local Home Assistant or MQTT loss and from failure of one cloud provider. Local safety evaluation shall remain available when only the WAN is lost, and outbound delivery shall follow the Local-Only policy in [LINK: SYS-SEC-3, §3] and §4.6. WAN monitoring shall establish reachability, not infer latency or packet-loss quality.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-008
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall monitor update information separately for Home Assistant Core, Operating System, Supervisor, and the SafetyComponent App when their update sources are available, retaining installed/offered versions, observation time, and source freshness. A confirmed available update shall be a level-3 warning and shall not by itself mean that the running safety function has failed; monitoring shall not install an update or restart a service.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-009
STATEMENT: >>>
A low battery in a still-functioning remote device shall be a level-4 informational maintenance condition, associated with one device identity even when several battery entities represent it. The system shall discover eligible device-associated battery entities from Home Assistant and allow the operator to exclude devices by stable device identity without manually enumerating all battery entities. When a device is excluded or removed, its obsolete maintenance-fault entity shall be retired without emitting a false clear; retirement shall persist across restarts. Failed discovery shall remain unknown coverage, not positive battery-health evidence or proof that devices were removed. An unavailable safety input shall retain the severity and owner of its separate coverage fault; battery status shall neither suppress nor clear that fault.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-010
STATEMENT: >>>
Each underlying failure shall have one fault owner. C-ENT shall retain entity-quality diagnostics and its existing per-entity faults; C-FSM shall own qualified platform and maintenance policy without duplicating a component-owned symptom.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-011
STATEMENT: >>>
System configuration shall provide threshold and qualification/recovery defaults; installation configuration may refine an explicit validated whitelist through component settings. The complete effective policy shall preserve recovery margins. Severity, freshness, technical scheduling and persistence shall remain system-owned. Installation configuration shall own source bindings and exclusions. Invalid configuration shall be rejected before enabling the affected contract. Missing or incompatible runtime sources shall be marked as uncovered, never silently reported healthy or used to disable unrelated safety mechanisms.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-012
STATEMENT: >>>
Platform and maintenance monitors shall provide observation and cause-specific manual inspection, repair or test-report tasks under §8.10. They shall issue no restart, update-installation or device-control commands without a separately assessed policy. Diagnostics and user-facing text shall preserve stable machine codes and equivalent EN/PL/DE meaning.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-013
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall present notification-route health and recovery-effect evidence separately from safety decisions. Home Assistant service acceptance shall not be described as delivery to a physical recipient, and an issued actuator command shall not be described as an achieved effect without the owning component's postcondition evidence. C-FSM shall aggregate these results without duplicating the notification or recovery owner's fault.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-014
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall track due dates and recorded outcomes of periodic tests for smoke, flammable-gas, and carbon-monoxide detectors. A test shall count as completed only after a device-reported result or an explicit operator attestation; absence of an alarm is not test evidence. A due, overdue or failed detector test shall produce a level-3 maintenance warning without suppressing a live detector alarm. Test intervals shall have system defaults and validated installation overrides of at most 180 days. Maintenance severity shall remain system-owned; detector binding and operator attestation are installation data.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-015
STATEMENT: >>>
Sustained low host free-disk-space and high host temperature shall create separate level-3 maintenance warnings after calibrated qualification. Each rule shall validate scope, units, plausibility, and freshness and require fresh recovery evidence beyond a distinct margin. Missing or invalid evidence shall not positively clear an active condition, and resource pressure alone shall not prove that a safety evaluation was missed.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-016
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall monitor the age of the last successful backup and an optional current backup-failure source. A valid success timestamp older than the calibrated maximum age or a valid asserted failure shall create a level-3 maintenance warning. Invalid, future, unavailable, or failed-transport evidence shall remain unknown rather than establish backup health. Backup creation evidence shall not establish restore capability, and monitoring shall neither create nor restore a backup.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-FSM-017
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall retain due dates and explicit operator-attested outcomes for notification-receipt tests and optional backup-restore tests. Home Assistant acceptance shall not complete a notification-receipt test; a backup-restore pass shall attest an actual restore on a separate installation. Due, overdue, and failed tests shall be level-4 maintenance conditions; unreadable history shall remain unknown. Recording results shall execute no notification, restore, actuator, or household routine. A separately confirmed operator action may send an identifiable mobile-only test without recording receipt; a separately confirmed full notification reset shall preserve active faults and local restrictions and reissue currently active warnings rather than suppress them. Installation configuration shall select tests and may refine interval defaults. Persistence and severity shall remain system-owned; interval changes shall not create completed tests.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
For **[LINK: SYS-SR-FSM-009, SYS-SR-FSM-009]**, battery freshness shall use the unique same-device
`sensor.*_last_seen`, normalized as `last_contact_at`, when that capability is
present. Recent device contact shall allow the last valid battery value without
a new battery report. Availability, units, device class, value plausibility and
reader transport validity shall remain required. Missing, invalid, future,
expired or ambiguous selected contact shall remain unknown without fallback
to native report times. Devices without contact capability shall retain native
report freshness. Both paths shall use the system-owned battery freshness bound;
polling shall not renew source age. System Health shall retain separate battery
maintenance and monitored-entity sections, with dynamic monitored counts.

C-NET remains the network/connectivity subdomain of C-FSM. C-ENT remains the
quality owner for declared Home Assistant input entities, including C-FSM's
telemetry dependencies where applicable. An unavailable safety input can coexist
with a host-resource or battery condition; neither state implies that a
household hazard has cleared.

---
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-8-9
TITLE: Water-Leak Monitoring (C-LEAK)

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Water-leak detection remains a distinct C-LEAK safety decision under
[LINK: HZ-WATER-01, HZ‑WATER‑01] / [LINK: SG-009, SG‑009] even when it shares the binary-detector runtime and
operator screen with C-ALARM. The input is an explicitly bound water-leak
sensor with a validated wet/dry state profile. It is not a smoke, gas or CO
signal. See the [LINK: ARCH-INTERNAL-ENVIRONMENTAL-HAZARD-MONITORING-DOC, Internal Environmental Hazard Monitoring architecture].
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-LEAK-001
STATEMENT: >>>
Each configured leak sensor shall retain its own detector identity, indoor area, source health and immediate wet-state symptom. One valid assertion shall create an independent L2 `WaterLeakDetected` fault and notify occupants within the [LINK: SG-009, SG‑009] response budget.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-009
  ROLE: Refines

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-LEAK-002
STATEMENT: >>>
An unavailable, malformed or ambiguous source shall not prove dry state or clear an active leak. Recovery shall require fresh authoritative dry evidence for the configured clear duration; another dry sensor shall not negate a wet sensor.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-LEAK-003
STATEMENT: >>>
The interface shall show leak alarm and source health separately and provide localized manual shutoff guidance. A leak binding alone shall not authorize valve operation; any automatic supply shutoff shall require an independently validated valve, installation binding and actuation policy.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-LEAK-004
STATEMENT: >>>
Leak-sensor functional tests shall require an explicit device result or operator attestation after an actual test, with system-owned intervals and maintenance severity. A test result shall not suppress a live leak alarm.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Other component allocations:** C-AQ retains
indoor-air-quality responsibilities beyond the PM2.5 allocation above;
intrusion/lock security by C-SEC, and Privacy by C-PRIV.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-8-10
TITLE: Recommended Actions and Recovery

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Recovery shall cover both hazard mitigation and restoration of failed monitoring
or supporting capabilities. The owning component shall specify the action and
its evidence; RecoveryManager shall own proposal lifecycle and command dispatch.
SafetyHome shall present the same action at its fault and in the shared list.
See [LINK: ARCH-RECOMMENDED-ACTIONS-AND-RECOVERY-DOC, Recommended Actions and Recovery architecture].
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-REC-001
STATEMENT: >>>
Each installed fault family shall have a cause-specific recovery allocation or an explicit rationale for no applicable action. Actions shall identify the fault/incident, cause, affected subject, purpose, instruction, executor, eligibility and completion method. Multiple causes shall retain independent actions without duplicate advice for the same failed path.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-REC-002
STATEMENT: >>>
Recovery shall support manual work, explicitly supported automatic commands and commands after user consent. Absence of an actuator or postcondition entity shall not prevent a manual action. A manual action shall issue no actuator command; fault urgency shall not authorize a command.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-REC-003
STATEMENT: >>>
Action completion shall be separate from fault healing, notification acknowledgement and command consent. Only the owning fault's qualified evidence and reset policy shall release its activation or restriction. Repairing one cause shall not clear another cause.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-REC-004
STATEMENT: >>>
Each completable task shall declare observed completion or an operator report for a procedural task; advisory instructions shall have no task-completion control. Reports shall retain identity derived from authenticated server context and server-assigned time, be bound to the current proposal/incident instance with a separate one-time report token, issue no actuator command and not substitute for required device evidence. Unknown actor context, replayed tokens and expired, cleared, shadowed or superseded instances shall be rejected; restart shall invalidate old report tokens. Approved maintenance-test reports may supply evidence only under the owning test's attestation policy. Reports of battery replacement shall await fresh recovered battery evidence; contacting service may complete the contact task while its fault remains active.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-REC-005
STATEMENT: >>>
Observed completion shall require all declared postconditions on current valid evidence. Missing, stale, unavailable or failed evidence shall remain unknown. Home Assistant command acceptance shall not establish physical effect, recipient receipt or thermal recovery.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-REC-006
STATEMENT: >>>
Proposals shall be reevaluated when relevant evidence, causes, policy or dependencies change. Superseded, expired, cleared, shadowed or ineligible executable proposals shall lose their authorization and stale guidance. Empty or rejected replacements shall not leave an old executable proposal active. Valid repair instructions shall remain available with visible reasons for blocked execution or unknown effect.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-REC-007
STATEMENT: >>>
Command authorization shall be revalidated immediately before execution using current evidence, dependencies, conflicts, configured actuator, expiry and replay protection. Unsupported commands and any actuator changes under manual policy shall be rejected centrally. Restart shall not replay a command.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-REC-008
STATEMENT: >>>
Distinct system-owned policies shall govern command-consent expiry, executed-command effect observation and optional action-specific manual-task deadlines. Installation bindings shall not set these deadlines, and manual service/battery work shall not inherit a short command-consent deadline. Active work, operator reports and bounded action outcomes shall survive restart, retaining their evidence source without treating saved reports or downtime as fresh physical evidence.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-REC-009
STATEMENT: >>>
The fault card, shared action list and notifications shall use consistent localized instructions and subject names. They shall distinguish user-reported work, observed completion, blocked execution, unknown effect, failure, withdrawal and no applicable action. EN/PL/DE text shall preserve equivalent meaning; stable identifiers and existing raw lifecycle codes shall remain unchanged.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-REC-010
STATEMENT: >>>
Manual hazard-response and equipment-repair actions shall obey the owning hazard/equipment guidance. Observation-only components shall offer useful manual actions without extending actuator permissions. Emergency guidance shall not require task acknowledgement, and detector clear or operator reports shall not authorize re-entry, gas reopening or safety-interlock bypass.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Verification shall cover manual closure, battery replacement with delayed
telemetry, unavailable-input repair, independent fault contributors, procedural
reports without sensors, command-policy enforcement, proposal withdrawal,
blocked-result presentation and restart without replay. HARA hazards and safety
goals shall retain their component allocation; offering manual actions shall not
claim that user response is guaranteed within a fault-tolerant time interval.

---
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-8-11
TITLE: Safety Routines (C-ROUTINE)

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Safety Routines shall support contextual user checks independently of continuous
hazard detection. The [LINK: ARCH-SAFETY-ROUTINES-DOC, feature architecture]
defines configuration, evidence, execution and report contracts.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-ROUTINE-001
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall execute a configured set of advisory checks on an explicit request or configured HA event/state transition.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-ROUTINE-002
STATEMENT: >>>
Checks shall qualify original observation times and expected states or numeric units and bounds. An explicitly configured single-sample check may compare the last known entity value against those expectations without qualifying its age. Its report shall identify that basis. Unavailable or malformed values and observations that fail a required freshness check shall remain unevaluable.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-ROUTINE-003
STATEMENT: >>>
Known exclusion conditions shall be distinguished from unknown applicability; an entirely skipped run shall not count as a pass.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-ROUTINE-004
STATEMENT: >>>
Each run shall produce an independently identifiable timestamped report preserving both detected problems and missing evidence. Later runs shall not rewrite completed reports.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-ROUTINE-005
STATEMENT: >>>
Execution, history and publication shall be bounded. Restart and cancellation shall preserve incomplete execution as uncertainty without replay.
<<<

[SYSTEM_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-SR-ROUTINE-006
STATEMENT: >>>
Routine results shall not set or clear household faults, dispatch actuators, substitute for continuous coverage or claim current safety from a historical result. Application storage failures shall remain visible separately.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-9
TITLE: Non‑Functional Requirements (NFR)

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
_Non‑functional constraints that apply across all components. IDs use `NFR‑xxx`._
<<<

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-9-1
TITLE: Performance & Timing

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-001
TITLE: Decision latency
STATEMENT: >>>
Safety decision path **p95 ≤ T_decision_max** and **p99 ≤ T_decision_max_p99** under peak input rate.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-002
TITLE: FTTI compliance
STATEMENT: >>>
For each SG, `T_detection + T_decision + T_recovery + T_effect ≤ FTTI` (see [LINK: SYS-SEC-6, §6]). Budget tracked per component.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-003
TITLE: Notification deadlines
STATEMENT: >>>
Deliver **L1 ≤ T_notify_L1**, **L2/L3 ≤ T_notify_L23**; **L4** best‑effort ([LINK: SYS-SEC-4, §4]).
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-004
TITLE: Actuation verification
STATEMENT: >>>
Read‑back state within **T_verify_actuation**; on mismatch → retry **N_retry** with cooldown **T_cooldown**.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-9-2
TITLE: Reliability & Availability

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-010
TITLE: Watchdog
STATEMENT: >>>
A software watchdog **shall** detect loop stalls ≥ **T_watchdog** and log **EVT_WATCHDOG**; optional auto‑recover per policy.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-011
TITLE: Data integrity
STATEMENT: >>>
Inputs lacking `ts/src` or failing CRC/format checks **shall** be rejected and logged as **Degraded**.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-012
TITLE: Persistence
STATEMENT: >>>
Evidence and config **shall** survive process restarts and power cycles (durable writes or journal).
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-013
TITLE: UPS posture
STATEMENT: >>>
On power failure detection, prefer local safe states (e.g., close valve, unlock door for fire) when feasible.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-014
TITLE: External isolation
STATEMENT: >>>
External API calls shall use bounded timeouts and independent schedules; no external call may block the AppDaemon decision path or another provider beyond `T_api_block_max`.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-015
TITLE: Last-known semantics
STATEMENT: >>>
Provider loss shall preserve the last observation with explicit age until its policy expiry; it shall never convert stale data into a false `clear` state.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-9-3
TITLE: Security (safety‑relevant)

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-020
TITLE: AuthN/AuthZ
STATEMENT: >>>
Administrative actions require **strong auth**; runtime actuation restricted by **RBAC**; audit all privileged operations.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-021
TITLE: Config integrity
STATEMENT: >>>
`backend/app_cfg.yaml` shall pass complete schema,
component, entity, and area validation before safety mechanisms are enabled.
A deployment shall hash-verify transferred application and configuration files;
validation failure shall publish `invalid_cfg` and leave safety mechanisms
disabled while diagnostics remain available.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-022
TITLE: Secrets handling
STATEMENT: >>>
Credentials stored via platform secrets; never in evidence logs.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-023
TITLE: Network posture
STATEMENT: >>>
Prefer **local control paths** for life‑safety; cloud paths treated as best‑effort.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-9-4
TITLE: Maintainability & Modularity

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-030
TITLE: Component isolation
STATEMENT: >>>
Safety components (C‑TEMP, C‑ALARM, …) **shall** expose clear inputs/outputs and not share mutable state (except via evidence/metrics).
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-031
TITLE: Freedom from interference
STATEMENT: >>>
Non‑safety automations **shall not** preempt or delay safety decisions/actuations beyond **T_fi_max**.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-032
TITLE: Feature flags
STATEMENT: >>>
New mechanisms (e.g., forecasting) start in **shadow mode** and only activate when **C_target** is met.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-033
TITLE: Provider isolation
STATEMENT: >>>
Each external API Component shall be replaceable and testable without importing provider-specific schemas into C-EXT or other API Components.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-9-5
TITLE: Observability & Evidence

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-040
TITLE: Evidence schema
STATEMENT: >>>
Each decision/command emits evidence `{ts, rule_id, inputs(min/max/avg), thresholds, debounce/suppression, action, result, latency_ms}` ([LINK: SYS-SEC-7-7, §7.7]).
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-041
TITLE: Metrics
STATEMENT: >>>
Export counters/gauges for `decisions_total{rule,decision}`, `suppression_active`, `decision_latency_ms` (p50/p95/p99), and per‑interface freshness.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-042
TITLE: Retention
STATEMENT: >>>
Keep evidence for **T_evidence_retention** (rolling); rotate files daily; protect against unbounded growth.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-9-6
TITLE: Usability & UX

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-050
TITLE: Acknowledgement
STATEMENT: >>>
L1–L3 notifications **shall** be acknowledgeable from UI; ack silences repeats but does not clear faults.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-051
TITLE: Accessibility
STATEMENT: >>>
Visual alerts (lights/UI) must be paired with audible alerts for L1 to support different user needs.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-9-7
TITLE: Portability & Configurability

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-060
TITLE: Config‑only tuning
STATEMENT: >>>
All thresholds/timers are parameters (no hard‑coding). Per‑room/zone overrides supported.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-061
TITLE: Hardware‑agnostic
STATEMENT: >>>
Interfaces adhere to [LINK: SYS-SEC-7, §7] contracts so any compliant sensor/actuator can be used.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-9-8
TITLE: Compliance & Testing

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-070
TITLE: Testability
STATEMENT: >>>
Each requirement maps to unit/integration/HIL tests; fault injection covers timeout, stuck‑at, stale, comms loss.
<<<

[NON_FUNCTIONAL_REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-071
TITLE: Drills
STATEMENT: >>>
Periodic household drills for L1 scenarios (fire/CO/gas) logged as **TEST** with opt‑out window.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
---
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-10
TITLE: Glossary

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
- **HARA:** Hazard Analysis and Risk Assessment used to derive SGs and FTTIs.
- **Safety Goal (SG):** Top‑level safety objective linked to a hazard with a required **FTTI** and **Safe State**.
- **FTTI:** Fault Tolerant Time Interval — max allowed time from fault occurrence to reaching the safe state.
- **Safe State (SS‑x):** Predefined state that reduces risk to an acceptable level (e.g., SS‑Alarm, SS‑1…SS‑5).
- **Prefault (PR‑xxx):** Early warning for a specific subject (room/sensor) prior to raising a consolidated Fault.
- **Fault (F‑xxx):** Aggregated, user‑visible condition comprising one or more prefaults with attributes.
- **Suppression window (S):** Minimum time between repeated alerts to avoid storming.
- **Hysteresis (H):** Band around a threshold preventing chatter.
- **Shadow mode:** Mechanism runs without actuation to measure precision before activation.
- **Read‑back verification:** Check that an actuator achieved the commanded state within **T_verify_actuation**.
- **Freedom from interference:** Assurance that non‑safety code cannot degrade safety behavior beyond **T_fi_max**.
- **Local‑Only mode:** Operation when WAN is down; use local actuations and queue cloud notifications.
- **Evidence:** Immutable log of decisions/commands enabling traceability and audits.
- **ROC:** Rate of Change check used in plausibility diagnostics.

---
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SYS-SEC-11
TITLE: System Parameter Reference

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
> Reference values are non-binding system policy inputs. Exact validated keys
> for C-TEMP, C-EXT, and C-DOOR are defined in [LINK: SYS-SEC-8-2-3, §8.2.3], the C-EXT architecture,
> and [LINK: SYS-SEC-8-4-2, §8.4.2] respectively.

| Name                     | Description                                  | Reference Value |
| ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- | ---------------- |
| **T_min**                | Minimum safe room temperature                | 17 °C (per room) |
| **T_max**                | Maximum safe room temperature                | 29 °C (per room) |
| **T_crit**               | Max duration below **T_min** ([LINK: SG-001, SG‑001])        | 10 min           |
| **T_crit_hot**           | Max duration above **T_max** ([LINK: SG-004, SG‑004])        | 5 min            |
| **T_det**                | Cold‑side detection window                   | 120–360 s        |
| **H**                    | Cold hysteresis band                         | 0.5–1.5 °C       |
| **S**                    | Cold suppression window                      | 60–180 s         |
| **T_det_hot**            | Hot‑side detection window                    | 60–180 s         |
| **H_hot**                | Hot hysteresis band                          | 0.5–1.5 °C       |
| **S_hot**                | Hot suppression window                       | 60–180 s         |
| **H_pred**               | Temperature forecast horizon                 | 5–15 min         |
| **C_min**                | Min forecast confidence (temp)               | 0.7–0.8          |
| **ΔT_min**               | Min forecast delta                           | 0.5–1.0 °C       |
| **T_timeout**            | Sensor/comm timeout (general)                | 30–60 s          |
| **dT/dt_max**            | Max allowed temp ROC                         | 2–5 °C/min       |
| **Δt_max**               | Max timebase error                           | 20–100 ms        |
| **f_sensor_min**         | Min sensor frequency (general)               | 0.2–1 Hz         |
| **T_stable**             | Stable time before clearing prefault/fault   | 5–15 min         |
| **T_notify_L1**          | L1 delivery deadline                         | ≤ 10 s           |
| **T_notify_L23**         | L2/L3 delivery deadline                      | ≤ 30 s           |
| **T_decision_max**       | Max decision compute time (p95)              | ≤ 100 ms         |
| **T_decision_max_p99**   | Max decision compute time (p99)              | ≤ 250 ms         |
| **C_target**             | Shadow precision KPI to activate forecasting | ≥ 70 %           |
| **T_timeout_aq**         | AQ stale timeout                             | 60–120 s         |
| **T_act_aq**             | AQ actuation deadline                        | ≤ 30 s           |
| **Leak_debounce**        | Debounce for leak sensors                    | 0.2–1.0 s        |
| **Valve_close_s**        | Water/gas valve close verification time      | ≤ 5 s            |
| **Window_close_start_s** | Start motion after close command             | ≤ 2 s            |
| **N_retry**              | Retries for actuation/notify                 | 1–3              |
| **T_cooldown**           | Cooldown between retries                     | 30–120 s         |
| **T_watchdog**           | Loop stall detection interval                | 2–5 s            |
| **T_evidence_retention** | Evidence retention period                    | 30–90 days       |
| **WAN_loss_threshold**   | Condition to enter Local‑Only (M4)           | WAN link = down  |
| **SMS_enabled**          | Enable SMS/cellular fallback                 | false            |
| **SMS_max_retries**      | Max SMS send retries                         | 3                |
| **SMS_cooldown_s**       | Cooldown between SMS retries                 | 60 s             |
| **T_ext_door_open**      | Continuous known-open duration before a new exposure through an external door, garage door, or gate | 60 s |
| **T_ext_decision**       | Decision budget after the applicable evidence and opening qualification are available | ≤ 1 s |
| **T_ext_clear**          | Stable positive evidence before exposure clear | 2–10 min       |
| **T_api_block_max**      | Maximum blocking time of one provider call   | ≤ 10 s           |
| **T_poll_weather**       | Open-Meteo weather poll interval             | 10 min           |
| **T_poll_imgw**          | IMGW warnings poll interval                  | 5 min            |
| **T_poll_aq_model**      | Open-Meteo current AQ model poll interval    | 30 min           |
| **T_stale_weather**      | Weather capability stale timeout             | 20 min           |
| **T_stale_warning**      | Official weather warning provider stale timeout | 15 min        |
| **T_stale_aq**           | Outdoor AQ capability stale timeout          | 45 min           |
| **T_frost_watch**        | Configurable external frost watch threshold  | 2 °C             |
| **T_frost_warning**      | Configurable external frost warning threshold | 0 °C            |
| **V_gust_watch**         | Configurable wind-gust watch threshold       | 15 m/s           |
| **V_gust_warning**       | Configurable wind-gust warning threshold     | 20 m/s           |

_All deployable parameter bindings live under `SafetyFunctions.app_config` or
`SafetyFunctions.user_config` in `backend/app_cfg.yaml` (see [LINK: SYS-SEC-4-5, §4.5]). A parameter
shall not affect runtime behavior until its owning component defines and
validates the corresponding configuration key._
